#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ade-scan-state: scan of bigteam task dirs and git checkouts that changes no work.

Phase 0 item 4.3 of docs/ade-everywhere-proposal.md (djbclark, 2026-10-20): find unique reports,
dirty trees, untracked files and unpushed commits before anything is cleaned up. Nothing is moved,
deleted, committed, stashed or checked out; the only network calls are `git ls-remote` (--remote)
and `git fetch` of one branch (--fetch) when the origin tip cannot be compared: it downloads that
branch's objects, writes no refs and no FETCH_HEAD, and runs no auto-maintenance. GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1
is set on every git call, so a partial clone never downloads objects as a side effect of a count
(the tip then stays uncompared: `origin tip not fetched`) unless --fetch asks for it.

A checkout's own git config can name commands (core.fsmonitor, core.sshCommand, remote.*.uploadpack,
filter.*, gpg.program with log.showSignature, credential.*, hooksPath, include.*, ...). The tool reads
that config without running anything first and accepts only an allowlist of keys (SAFE_CONFIG below);
a checkout with any other key is `skipped: unrecognised config <key>`, one with core.worktree or a
work tree that is not the scanned dir is `skipped: worktree redirected`. The same allowlist is applied
to every submodule's config under the git dir (`skipped: submodule config unrecognised <key>`).
Every later git call also turns fsmonitor, hooks and signature display off, drops every GIT_*
variable from the environment, reads no global or system config, ignores submodules in `status`
(so a checkout with submodules carries the flag `submodules not inspected`, never a bare
`clean and pushed`), counts stashes from the reflog file, and `ls-remote` runs from a neutral
directory. Symlinks out of a --git-root are not followed: name each root you want scanned.

  ade-scan-state --bigteam ~/.local/state/bigteam \\
      --ref-root ~/src/djbclark-ade/docs --ref-root ~/ops/site-private/memory \\
      --ref-root ~/.local/state/handoffs \\
      --exclude 'extra-doc-*.md' \\
      --git-root ~/orca/workspaces --git-root ~/orca/projects --git-root ~/.cow/pastures \\
      --remote --json > scan.json
  ade-scan-state --bigteam DIR --markdown        # raw tables (bin/ade-scan-state-doc builds the doc)

`ade-everywhere-phase0-scan-*` is always excluded from the reference search (the scan doc names every
task it lists, so it would make every report look cited); --exclude adds globs, it never replaces it.

Bigteam verdicts (per task dir, ~/.local/state/bigteam/<task>/):
  scratch  no report file, or every report is already referenced under a --ref-root
  unique   an unreferenced report file and the dir is at most --unique-max-bytes (5 MiB): the
           reports are the dir
  mixed    an unreferenced report file inside a larger dir (logs, worktrees, caches)
A "report file" is *report*.md (not *.done) within three levels of the dir, not inside a git
checkout. "Referenced" is a whole-word match of the task name in any file under a --ref-root
(`rg -l -F -w`, or `grep -rlFw` without rg); a short common name can match by accident, so a
referenced verdict is a lead, not a proof. `size` is `du -sk`; `newest_mtime` looks two levels deep.
  scan incomplete: du rc=<n>  `du` exited nonzero (an unreadable subtree: it still prints a partial total,
           which must not decide `unique` against `mixed`): same rule, `traversal: "incomplete: du rc=<n>"`
           unless a `scandir` failure is also present (that one is named); `bytes` stays as `du` printed it.
  scan incomplete: reference search rc=<n>  the `rg`/`grep` search exited above 1 (an error) or timed
           out (rc=124): the task is never `unique`, `mixed` or `scratch`, its record has
           `reference_search: "incomplete: rc=<n>"` (null otherwise), and the summary counts it under
           `bigteam.incomplete`. The exit status stays 0, as for an incomplete checkout.
  scan incomplete: scandir <ERRNO> <relative path>  a subtree of the task could not be listed, or its
           `CLAIM` or `jobs` could not be read or listed (it may hold the only report): same rule, the
           record has `traversal: "incomplete: scandir ..."` (null otherwise; the first failure by
           path), counted under `bigteam.incomplete`.

A --git-root or --ref-root that is not a directory (a --ref-root must also be searchable), and a --bigteam
that is not a directory or cannot be listed, is a usage error (exit 2) before anything is scanned.

Git checkout verdicts (a checkout is a dir holding `.git`, found up to two levels under each
--git-root, a repository nested inside a checkout included, symlinks not followed, deduplicated):
  unpushed commits  the upstream exists and HEAD is ahead of it; or origin lists the branch and HEAD
                    is ahead; or any local branch (or a detached HEAD) holds a commit that is on no
                    origin ref, whatever the origin state
  orphan branch     the branch is on no origin ref and HEAD has commits origin lacks
  dirty             modified or untracked non-ignored paths
  clean and pushed  none of the above: no commit on any local branch, local tag or on HEAD lies outside the
                    origin refs (the local `refs/remotes/origin/*`; with --remote, only origin's own live heads and tags)
  skipped: <reason>  a config key outside the allowlist, a redirected work tree, an unreadable
                    config or an unreadable `.git/HEAD` (`skipped: HEAD unreadable`: git does not
                    recognise the repository); nothing else was run
  scan incomplete: <call> rc=<n>  a git call (`status`, `rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`, `rev-list
                    --count ...`, `for-each-ref`, `remote get-url origin`: only exit 2 "No such remote" means no origin) failed or timed out (rc=124); the state is unknown,
                    so the checkout is never `clean and pushed`; counts are null. `git status` gets
                    600 s, every other git call 60 s. A repository counts as unborn (no commit yet) only
                    when `refs/heads` and `refs/tags` hold nothing; a HEAD that names no ref while
                    refs exist is `scan incomplete: rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD rc=<n>`.
  scan incomplete: scandir <ERRNO>  a directory inside a --git-root could not be listed, or its `.git`
                    could not be inspected (a directory that is listable but not searchable), so
                    checkouts below it may be missing: one record for that dir (path, found_via,
                    counts null), counted under `checkouts.incomplete`; the exit status stays 0.
Local-only commits: `local_only_commits` is `git rev-list --count HEAD --branches --tags --not
--remotes=origin` (a commit reachable only from a local tag counts: the branch it was on may be reset or
deleted; with no origin remote nothing is excluded, a leftover `refs/remotes/origin/*` ref proves
nothing); with --remote and an answering `ls-remote` it is `--not <every live origin head and tag tip>`
alone (a tag origin has is not local-only; an annotated tag contributes its tag object and its peeled
commit): a stale local tracking ref of a live branch proves nothing (origin may have force-pushed it) and
one of a deleted branch is stale (a live tip whose objects are missing here excludes nothing, so the
count can only overcount; --fetch downloads those tips first). `local_only_verified` is true only when
--remote got a live `ls-remote` answer and no live tip is missing; false when it did and tips are still
missing, i.e. the count is an upper bound, and also when --remote asked and origin did not answer (the
count then trusts the local `refs/remotes/origin/*`, the only exclusion left: a stale tracking ref of a
deleted or force-pushed branch hides commits, so the count is an undercount); null (JSON) when --remote
was not given (the same trust in the local refs, a documented caveat, nothing was asked) and for a skipped
checkout. When it is above 0, `local_only_branches` is a
list of {branch, count}, one `rev-list --count` per local branch (a detached HEAD appears as branch `HEAD`),
then `local_only_tags` a list of {tag, count}, one per local tag; at most 100 refs are counted in all
(branches first), the rest in `local_only_branches_uncounted`: one extra git call per
checkout when nothing is local-only and at most 104 otherwise. The summary counts these as `with_local_only_commits`.
Extra flag `local-only commits: <branch> <n>, tag <tag> <n>, ...`: the same lists, first five refs.
Extra flag `detached`: HEAD is detached and commits lie outside origin; `origin unknown`: the branch
is not detached but origin's branch list could not be read at all (the verdict is `unpushed commits`,
never `clean and pushed`).
Primary flag `origin unreachable: local-only count unverified` (--remote only): `ls-remote` failed, so the
local `refs/remotes/origin/*` stood in for it; the checkout is never `clean and pushed` (`local_only_verified`
false), whatever the local refs say, since a stale tracking ref would hide commits.
Primary flag `origin url holds a credential: not queried` (--remote only): the first `remote.origin.url` has a
password, or (any scheme but ssh) a username or a query string (`https://user:secret@host/x`,
`https://<token>@github.com/x`, `https://host/x?private_token=...`; `ssh://git@host` and scp-like
`git@host:x` are usernames git never hands to a helper, so they are none; a `<transport>::<address>` prefix is
stripped and the address judged), so `ls-remote` is not run and `--fetch` does not fetch; the checkout is treated exactly like `origin
unreachable` (never `clean and pushed`, `local_only_verified` false). The url is in no record, flag, message
or doc, and the query cache is keyed by its sha256 prefix. For any other url `ls-remote` is run as `git
ls-remote --heads --tags -- origin` with the url in GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1, GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=remote.origin.url,
GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=<url> (git 2.31+): never in argv, where any process could read it; an older git is not
asked (`origin unreachable`, one line on stderr). `--fetch` runs inside the checkout as `fetch origin`.
Extra flag `submodules not inspected`: the checkout has submodules; `status` ignores their state, so
the verdict says nothing about them (it is that flag, not `clean and pushed`, when nothing else applies)
and the capture plan always lists the checkout with an instruction to scan each submodule as its own checkout.
Extra flag `origin tip not fetched`: the branch is on origin at a commit this checkout cannot
compare with HEAD (add --fetch); `ahead_of_origin` then still counts those commits.
Flag `origin tips not fetched: <n>` (--remote): n live origin tips whose objects are not here (the
checkout is behind origin, or origin was force-pushed, on those branches; add --fetch). It is a primary
flag: the verdict is never `clean and pushed` while any is missing. Presence and type come from `git cat-file
--batch-check`: a tag whose peeled object is a tree or a blob (git.git `junio-gpg-pub`, linux `v2.6.11-tree`)
reaches no commit, so it is neither counted missing nor fetched (a tip whose commit is really absent still
counts, and --fetch clears it).
--remote trusts origin's answer as given: a lying origin can hide any local commit by naming it as a branch,
tag or peeled tip.
Flag `origin advertised invalid ref names: <n>` (--remote, `invalid_origin_refs` in the record): n entries
of origin's answer dropped because their name is not one git creates (`:`, `..`, a control character,
...) or their object id is malformed or all zeros. They are never used in a fetch refspec, so a hostile
origin cannot make --fetch write a local ref. It is a primary flag: never `clean and pushed`.
A checkout with no `origin` remote at all has every commit "ahead of origin": it is an orphan
branch and carries the extra flag `no origin remote`. `stash`, `no origin remote`, `detached` and
`origin unknown` are flags, never a primary verdict.
The primary verdict is the first that applies in that order; `flags` lists every one. A skipped
checkout has no counts (null) and `config_keys` names the offending keys, never their values. `--markdown`
cells show every control character (newline, a bare CR, tab, ...) as backslash text, never the raw byte.

Exit codes: 0 scan completed; 2 usage error. Pass --json for machine-readable output.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import errno
import json
import os
import re
import atexit
import hashlib
import shutil
import signal
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
from pathlib import Path
from urllib.parse import urlsplit

REPORT_DEPTH = 3
MTIME_DEPTH = 2
GIT_SCAN_DEPTH = 2
SKIP_DIRS = {".git", "node_modules", "__pycache__", ".venv", "target"}
# Always excluded from the "is this task name cited" search; --exclude adds to this list.
DEFAULT_EXCLUDES = ["ade-everywhere-phase0-scan-*"]
SKIP_UNREADABLE = "skipped: git config unreadable"
SKIP_WORKTREE = "skipped: worktree redirected"
SKIP_NO_WORKTREE = "skipped: no work tree"
SUBMODULES_FLAG = "submodules not inspected"
SKIP_HEAD_UNREADABLE = "skipped: HEAD unreadable"
MAX_TIPS = 2000  # ls-remote tips passed as `--not` arguments (argv size); extra tips only overcount
MAX_BRANCH_COUNTS = 100  # `rev-list --count` calls for the per-branch report; the rest are only summed
# Flags that qualify a verdict but never are one.
INFO_FLAGS = ("stash", "no origin remote", "detached", "origin unknown")
ORIGIN_UNREACHABLE = "origin unreachable: local-only count unverified"  # primary: --remote got no answer
ORIGIN_CREDENTIAL = "origin url holds a credential: not queried"  # primary: treated like ORIGIN_UNREACHABLE
MIN_GIT_ENV_CONFIG = (2, 31)  # GIT_CONFIG_COUNT / _KEY_n / _VALUE_n: how `ls-remote` gets the url
TIPS_NOT_FETCHED = "origin tips not fetched: "  # flag + count: live tips whose objects are not here
INVALID_REFS = "origin advertised invalid ref names: "  # flag + count: ls-remote entries git would refuse
INCOMPLETE_PREFIX = "scan incomplete: "
STATUS_TIMEOUT = 600  # `git status` on a large or cold tree under load; every other git call gets 60 s
NO_SUCH_REMOTE_RC = 2  # `git remote get-url origin` on a repository without origin: exit 2, "error: No such remote 'origin'"
MODULE_INTERNAL_DIRS = ("objects", "refs", "logs", "hooks", "lfs")  # pruned inside a module's git dir
# Prefix of every git call: no index lock, and the config-named programs that run on any command off
# (log.showSignature=false: `stash list` / `log` would run gpg.program on a signed commit otherwise).
GIT_SAFE = ["--no-optional-locks", "-c", "core.fsmonitor=false", "-c", "core.hooksPath=/dev/null",
            "-c", "protocol.ext.allow=never", "-c", "log.showSignature=false"]
# Allowlist of the checkout's own config (local and worktree scope), 2026-10-20 (adversary round 2,
# R2-1: the deny list missed three paths in two rounds). Keys are lower-cased on section and variable,
# the subsection is kept verbatim. Anything else, `core.worktree` included, makes the checkout skipped.
# None of these runs a program: no gpg.*, filter.*, diff.*, credential.*, alias.*, url.*, include.*,
# core.sshCommand / hooksPath / pager / editor, fetch.recurseSubmodules, remote.*.uploadpack.
SAFE_CONFIG = frozenset({
    "core.repositoryformatversion", "core.filemode", "core.bare", "core.logallrefupdates",
    "core.ignorecase", "core.precomposeunicode", "core.symlinks", "core.autocrlf", "core.eol",
    "core.safecrlf", "core.quotepath", "core.excludesfile", "core.sparsecheckout",
    "core.sparsecheckoutcone", "core.untrackedcache", "core.fsmonitor",  # fsmonitor: true/false only
    "user.name", "user.email", "user.signingkey", "commit.gpgsign",  # a key id: executes nothing
    "extensions.worktreeconfig", "extensions.objectformat", "extensions.refstorage",
    "submodule.active", "pull.rebase", "pull.ff", "push.default", "push.autosetupremote",
    "merge.conflictstyle", "rerere.enabled", "status.showuntrackedfiles",  # status gets the flag too
    "lfs.repositoryformatversion"})
# section -> variables allowed under any subsection (remote.<name>.url, branch.<name>.merge, ...)
SAFE_SUBSECTION_CONFIG = {
    "remote": frozenset({"url", "pushurl", "fetch", "tagopt", "prune", "promisor",
                         "partialclonefilter", "gh-resolved"}),
    "branch": frozenset({"remote", "merge", "rebase", "pushremote", "gh-merge-base",
                         "vscode-merge-base"}),
    "submodule": frozenset({"url", "active", "ignore"}),
    "lfs": frozenset({"access"})}


def git_env(network: bool = False) -> dict:
    """The caller's environment minus every GIT_* key, plus this tool's own; no global/system config."""
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}
    # GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH (git 2.45+): a partial clone must not download objects on a count/compare;
    # it stays set for the explicit --fetch too (that one is a real fetch and needs no lazy help).
    env.update({"GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT": "0", "GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0", "GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM": "1",
                "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL": os.devnull, "GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH": "1", "LC_ALL": "C"})
    if network:
        env.pop("SSH_ASKPASS", None)
        env.update({"GIT_SSH_COMMAND": "ssh -o BatchMode=yes",
                    "GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL": "file:git:http:https:ssh"})
    return env


def run(argv, timeout=120, cwd=None, env=None, stdin_text=None):
    """argv list in, (rc, stdout); never raises on a missing tool or timeout.

    stdin is /dev/null (or `stdin_text`, written whole) and the child leads its own process group, so a
    timeout kills git together with the ssh / git-remote-https children it started."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.Popen(argv, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL if stdin_text is None else subprocess.PIPE,
                             stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, errors="replace",
                             cwd=cwd, env=env, start_new_session=True)
    except OSError:
        return 124, ""
    try:
        out, _ = p.communicate(input=stdin_text, timeout=timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        try:
            os.killpg(p.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        except OSError:
            pass
        p.communicate()
        return 124, ""
    return p.returncode, out


def du_bytes(path: Path) -> tuple[int, int | None]:
    """(bytes as reported, None) or (bytes as reported, rc) when `du` failed: an unreadable subtree makes
    `du` exit nonzero yet print a partial total, so only rc 0 is a complete number (Codex pass 6)."""
    rc, out = run(["du", "-sk", str(path)], timeout=900)
    nbytes = 0
    if out.split():
        try:
            nbytes = int(out.split()[0]) * 1024
        except ValueError:
            if rc == 0:
                rc = 65  # exited 0 but printed no size: not an answer
    elif rc == 0:
        rc = 65
    return nbytes, (rc or None)


def is_checkout(path: Path) -> bool:
    """True when `path` holds a `.git` that is not a symlink. lstat is used instead of `Path.exists()`,
    which since Python 3.13 turns any OSError into False: only ENOENT/ENOTDIR mean "no .git here";
    any other error (EACCES on a directory that is listable but not searchable) is raised."""
    try:
        mode = os.lstat(path / ".git").st_mode
    except (FileNotFoundError, NotADirectoryError):
        return False
    return not stat.S_ISLNK(mode)


def errno_name(exc: OSError) -> str:
    return errno.errorcode.get(exc.errno, "EIO") if exc.errno else "EIO"


def walk(path: Path, depth: int, skip_checkouts: bool = True,
         failures: list[tuple[Path, str]] | None = None):
    """Yield file paths up to `depth` levels below path.

    A directory that cannot be listed is appended to `failures` as (path, errno name) when the caller
    passes a list; it is never silently dropped from what the caller concludes (adversary/Codex pass 5)."""
    try:
        entries = list(os.scandir(path))
    except OSError as exc:
        if failures is not None:
            failures.append((Path(path), errno_name(exc)))
        return
    for e in entries:
        try:
            if e.is_symlink():
                continue
            if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
                if e.name in SKIP_DIRS or depth <= 1:
                    continue
                if skip_checkouts and is_checkout(Path(e.path)):
                    continue
                yield from walk(Path(e.path), depth - 1, skip_checkouts, failures)
            elif e.is_file(follow_symlinks=False):
                yield Path(e.path)
        except OSError as exc:
            if failures is not None:
                failures.append((Path(e.path), errno_name(exc)))


def newest_mtime(path: Path) -> float:
    best = 0.0
    try:
        best = path.stat().st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return best
    for f in walk(path, MTIME_DEPTH + 1, skip_checkouts=False):
        try:
            best = max(best, f.lstat().st_mtime)
        except OSError:
            pass
    try:
        for e in os.scandir(path):
            try:
                best = max(best, e.stat(follow_symlinks=False).st_mtime)
            except OSError:
                pass
    except OSError:
        pass
    return best


def claim_state(task: Path, failures: list[tuple[Path, str]] | None = None) -> str:
    claim = task / "CLAIM"
    try:
        lines = [ln.strip() for ln in claim.read_text(errors="replace").splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    except (FileNotFoundError, NotADirectoryError):
        return "no CLAIM"
    except OSError as exc:
        if failures is not None:
            failures.append((claim, errno_name(exc)))
        return "no CLAIM"
    return "DONE" if lines and lines[-1].startswith("DONE") else "open"


def open_job_records(task: Path, failures: list[tuple[Path, str]] | None = None) -> list[str]:
    jobs = task / "jobs"
    try:
        names = sorted(e.name for e in os.scandir(jobs) if e.name.endswith(".json"))
    except (FileNotFoundError, NotADirectoryError):
        return []
    except OSError as exc:
        if failures is not None:
            failures.append((jobs, errno_name(exc)))
        return []
    out = []
    for name in names:
        try:
            data = json.loads((jobs / name).read_text(errors="replace"))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            out.append(f"{name} (unreadable)")
            continue
        if isinstance(data, dict) and "closed" not in data:
            out.append(name)
    return out


def report_files(task: Path, failures: list[tuple[Path, str]] | None = None) -> list[dict]:
    found = []
    for f in walk(task, REPORT_DEPTH + 1, failures=failures):
        n = f.name
        if n.endswith(".md") and "report" in n.lower():
            try:
                size = f.stat().st_size
            except OSError as exc:
                if failures is not None:
                    failures.append((f, errno_name(exc)))
                continue
            found.append({"path": str(f.relative_to(task)), "bytes": size,
                          "done": Path(str(f) + ".done").exists()})
    return sorted(found, key=lambda r: r["path"])


def referenced(name: str, ref_roots: list[Path], exclude: list[str]) -> tuple[list[str], int | None]:
    """(files under a ref root that mention `name`, None) or ([], rc) when the search itself failed.

    rg and grep exit 1 for "no match" and above 1 for an error (an unreadable root, a bad pattern);
    `run` returns 124 for a timeout or a missing tool. Only 0 and 1 are answers."""
    # every --ref-root was checked to be a listable directory in main(); one that vanished since
    # makes rg/grep exit 2, which is `scan incomplete`, never a silent drop from the search
    roots = [str(r) for r in ref_roots]
    if not roots:
        return [], None
    globs = [*DEFAULT_EXCLUDES, *exclude]
    if shutil.which("rg"):
        argv = ["rg", "-l", "-F", "-w", "--no-messages"]
        for g in globs:
            argv += ["-g", f"!{g}"]
        argv += ["--", name, *roots]
    else:
        argv = ["grep", "-rlFw", *[f"--exclude={g}" for g in globs], "--", name, *roots]
    rc, out = run(argv, timeout=120)
    if rc == 0:
        return out.split("\n")[:-1], None
    return ([], None) if rc == 1 else ([], rc)


def top_children(task: Path, n: int = 4) -> list[dict]:
    kids = []
    try:
        entries = list(os.scandir(task))
    except OSError:
        return kids
    for e in entries:
        kids.append({"name": e.name + ("/" if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False) else ""),
                     "bytes": du_bytes(Path(e.path))[0]})
    return sorted(kids, key=lambda k: -k["bytes"])[:n]


def scan_bigteam(root: Path, ref_roots: list[Path], exclude: list[str], unique_max: int,
                 top: int) -> dict:
    tasks = []
    for e in sorted(os.scandir(root), key=lambda e: e.name):
        if not e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):
            continue
        task = Path(e.path)
        size, du_rc = du_bytes(task)
        traversal: list[tuple[Path, str]] = []
        reports = report_files(task, traversal)
        claim = claim_state(task, traversal)
        open_jobs = open_job_records(task, traversal)
        refs, search_rc = referenced(task.name, ref_roots, exclude) if reports else ([], None)
        traversal_note = None
        if traversal or du_rc is not None:
            # a subtree that could not be listed may hold the only report, and a `du` that failed
            # printed a partial total that could slip under --unique-max-bytes: never `scratch`/`unique`/`mixed`
            if traversal:
                bad, err = min(traversal)  # scandir order is arbitrary: the first by path
                where = " ".join(os.path.relpath(bad, task).split("\n"))
                reason = f"scandir {err} {where}"
            else:
                reason = f"du rc={du_rc}"
            traversal_note = f"incomplete: {reason}"
            verdict, unique_bytes = f"{INCOMPLETE_PREFIX}{reason}", 0
        elif search_rc is not None:
            # the search did not run to an answer: never read it as "unreferenced" (unique/mixed)
            # or as "nothing to keep" (scratch)
            verdict, unique_bytes = f"{INCOMPLETE_PREFIX}reference search rc={search_rc}", 0
        elif not reports or refs:
            verdict, unique_bytes = "scratch", 0
        else:
            unique_bytes = sum(r["bytes"] for r in reports)
            verdict = "unique" if size <= unique_max else "mixed"
        tasks.append({
            "task": task.name, "bytes": size, "newest_mtime": newest_mtime(task),
            "claim": claim, "reports": reports,
            "open_job_records": open_jobs, "referenced_in": refs[:5],
            "verdict": verdict, "unique_bytes": unique_bytes,
            "reference_search": None if search_rc is None else f"incomplete: rc={search_rc}",
            "traversal": traversal_note,
        })
    for t in sorted(tasks, key=lambda t: -t["bytes"])[:top]:
        t["fills"] = top_children(root / t["task"])
    loose = [e.name for e in os.scandir(root) if not e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False)]
    # abspath, not resolve: the rows rendered from this must not depend on the cwd of the scan (adversary
    # round 20, LOW 4b); symlinks stay as typed
    return {"root": os.path.abspath(root), "tasks": tasks, "loose_files": loose}


def find_checkouts(root: Path, failures: list[tuple[Path, str]] | None = None) -> list[Path]:
    """Checkouts up to GIT_SCAN_DEPTH levels under `root`, repositories nested inside a checkout included
    (not its `.git` or dependency directories); a directory that cannot be listed goes to `failures` as
    (path, errno name) instead of reading as "no checkouts"."""
    found = []

    def fail(p: Path, exc: OSError):
        if failures is not None:
            failures.append((p, errno_name(exc)))

    def rec(p: Path, depth: int):
        try:
            checkout = is_checkout(p)
        except OSError as exc:  # .git cannot be inspected: a checkout in here may be missing
            fail(p, exc)
            return
        if checkout:
            found.append(p)
            # and keep going: an ordinary repository nested inside a checkout (not a submodule, which is a
            # `.git` file plus `core.worktree` and is skipped as such) holds its own work (Codex pass 22 on PR 27)
        if depth == 0:
            return
        try:
            kids = sorted(os.scandir(p), key=lambda e: e.name)
        except OSError as exc:
            fail(p, exc)
            return
        for e in kids:
            if checkout and e.name in SKIP_DIRS:
                continue  # `.git` itself and dependency trees inside a checkout
            try:
                if e.is_dir(follow_symlinks=False):  # a link may lead anywhere: name that root instead
                    rec(Path(e.path), depth - 1)
            except OSError as exc:
                fail(Path(e.path), exc)

    rec(root, GIT_SCAN_DEPTH)
    return found


def git(path: Path, *args, timeout=60, network=False, stdin_text=None):
    kw = {} if stdin_text is None else {"stdin_text": stdin_text}
    return run(["git", *GIT_SAFE, "-C", str(path), *args], timeout=timeout, env=git_env(network), **kw)


def key_is_safe(key: str, value: str, in_submodule: bool = False) -> bool:
    """True when this config entry is on the allowlist (see SAFE_CONFIG)."""
    parts = key.split(".")
    section, var = parts[0].lower(), parts[-1].lower()
    if len(parts) < 2:
        return False
    if section == "core" and var == "fsmonitor":
        return len(parts) == 2 and value in ("true", "false")
    if in_submodule and key.lower() == "core.worktree":  # git itself writes it into every module
        return True
    if len(parts) == 2:
        return f"{section}.{var}" in SAFE_CONFIG
    return var in SAFE_SUBSECTION_CONFIG.get(section, ())


def config_offenders(entries, in_submodule: bool = False) -> list[str]:
    """Names (never values) of the entries that are not on the allowlist, first seen first."""
    bad: list[str] = []
    for key, value in entries:
        if not key_is_safe(key, value, in_submodule):
            first, *middle, last = key.split(".") if "." in key else [key, key]
            name = ".".join([first.lower(), *middle, last.lower()]) if "." in key else key
            if name not in bad:
                bad.append(name)
    return bad


def unsafe_config(path: Path) -> list[str] | None:
    """Names of the checkout's own config keys (local and worktree scope) that are not on the
    allowlist, never their values; None when the config cannot be read. Reading it executes nothing."""
    rc, out = git(path, "config", "--list", "--show-scope", "--includes", "-z")
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    tokens = out.split("\0")
    entries = []
    for scope, entry in zip(tokens[0::2], tokens[1::2]):
        if scope in ("local", "worktree"):
            key, _, value = entry.partition("\n")
            entries.append((key, value))
    return config_offenders(entries)


def git_common_dir(path: Path) -> Path | None:
    rc, out = git(path, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir")
    return (path / out.strip()).resolve() if rc == 0 and out.strip() else None


def submodule_config_check(path: Path, common: Path | None) -> tuple[bool, str | None]:
    """(has submodules, first config key of any submodule's git dir outside the allowlist).

    Reads `<git-common-dir>/modules/**/config` with `git config --file`, which executes nothing."""
    modules = common / "modules" if common else None
    present = (path / ".gitmodules").exists() or bool(modules and modules.is_dir())
    if not (present and modules and modules.is_dir()):
        return present, None
    env = git_env()
    env["GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES"] = str(Path(neutral_cwd()).parent)
    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(modules, followlinks=False):
        if "HEAD" in files or "config" in files:  # a module's git dir: skip its object store and logs
            dirs[:] = sorted(d for d in dirs if d not in MODULE_INTERNAL_DIRS)
        else:  # `modules/` itself or a dir that only groups nested modules: every name is a module
            dirs[:] = sorted(dirs)
        cfg = Path(root) / "config"
        if "config" not in files or cfg.is_symlink():
            continue
        rc, out = run(["git", *GIT_SAFE, "config", "--file", str(cfg), "--list", "-z"], timeout=60,
                      cwd=neutral_cwd(), env=env)
        if rc != 0:
            return present, "(unreadable)"
        entries = [tuple(e.partition("\n")[::2]) for e in out.split("\0") if e]
        bad = config_offenders(entries, in_submodule=True)
        if bad:
            return present, bad[0]
    return present, None


def stash_count(path: Path, common: Path | None) -> int | None:
    """Stashes = lines of the stash reflog file; no log machinery (no gpg, no pager) is involved. None when
    the count could not be taken (an unreadable reflog, a failed `stash list`): the caller records the scan as
    incomplete rather than inventing a count (Codex pass 22 on PR 27)."""
    if common:
        try:
            return len((common / "logs" / "refs" / "stash").read_bytes().splitlines())
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
        except OSError:
            return None
    rc, _ = git(path, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "refs/stash")
    if rc != 0:
        return 0
    rc, n = git(path, "stash", "list", "--format=%H")  # reftable: no reflog file; log.showSignature is off
    return max(len(n.splitlines()), 1) if rc == 0 else None


_neutral_cwd: list[str] = []


def neutral_cwd() -> str:
    """An empty dir outside any repo: ls-remote started here loads no checkout's config."""
    if not _neutral_cwd:
        d = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="ade-scan-state-")
        atexit.register(lambda: shutil.rmtree(d, ignore_errors=True))
        _neutral_cwd.append(d)
    return _neutral_cwd[0]


def queried_origin_url(path: Path) -> str | None:
    """The url `ls-remote` is given: the FIRST `remote.origin.url` in config order, which is what `git fetch
    origin` and `git ls-remote origin` use (`config --get` would print the last: a second url in the same
    file, or a worktree-scoped one after the local one, is not fetched from). A url that is a path (no
    scheme, no `:`) is relative to the checkout, not to our neutral cwd. None when it is unset or shaped
    like an option."""
    rc, out = git(path, "config", "--get-all", "remote.origin.url")
    url = out.splitlines()[0].strip() if rc == 0 and out.strip() else ""
    if not url or url.startswith("-"):
        return None
    if not os.path.isabs(url) and (url.startswith(".") or ":" not in url):
        url = str((path / url).resolve())
    return url


_BAD_REF_CHARS = re.compile(r"[\x00-\x20\x7f~^:?*\[\\]")
_OID = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{40}|[0-9a-f]{64}")


def valid_branch_name(name: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `refs/heads/<name>` passes `git check-ref-format` (plus: `@` alone is refused, as for a
    branch). origin is not trusted to advertise only names git itself would create: upload-pack lists
    `refs/heads/x:refs/heads/side` as it finds it on disk, and such a name pasted into a fetch refspec
    writes to its destination (adversary round 17, 1)."""
    if not name or name == "@" or name.endswith(("/", ".")):
        return False
    if _BAD_REF_CHARS.search(name) or ".." in name or "@{" in name:
        return False
    return all(part and not part.startswith(".") and not part.endswith(".lock")
               for part in name.split("/"))


def origin_url_env(env: dict, url: str) -> dict:
    """`env` plus the url as `remote.origin.url` through GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY_0/VALUE_0 (git 2.31+): the
    url never sits in a command line, where any process could read it for as long as the call runs
    (Codex pass 16). The call then names the remote `origin`, not the url."""
    return {**env, "GIT_CONFIG_COUNT": "1", "GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0": "remote.origin.url", "GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0": url}


_env_config_ok: list[bool] = []


def git_takes_env_config() -> bool:
    """Whether this git reads GIT_CONFIG_COUNT (2.31+). False when the version cannot be read or is older:
    the query is then refused (the checkout is `origin unreachable`), never made with the url in argv."""
    if not _env_config_ok:
        rc, out = run(["git", "--version"], timeout=60, env=git_env())
        m = re.search(r"(\d+)\.(\d+)", out) if rc == 0 else None
        if not m:
            return False
        _env_config_ok.append((int(m[1]), int(m[2])) >= MIN_GIT_ENV_CONFIG)
        if not _env_config_ok[0]:
            print(f"ade-scan-state: git {m[1]}.{m[2]} is older than {MIN_GIT_ENV_CONFIG[0]}.{MIN_GIT_ENV_CONFIG[1]}: "
                  "--remote does not query origin (the url would have to sit in argv)", file=sys.stderr)
    return _env_config_ok[0]


def url_holds_credential(url: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `url` may carry a credential git would hand to a transport helper's argv. A scp-like
    `host:path` (no `://`) is ssh and has none; `ssh://git@host` is a username, not a credential, but any
    password (`ssh://git:secret@host`) is one. For every other scheme (http, https, ...) ANY userinfo counts
    (`https://<token>@github.com/x` is how a token is used as the username; adversary round 22) and so does
    a query string (`?private_token=...`); a `<transport>::<address>` prefix names a helper that gets the whole
    address in argv, so any `@` or `?` in it counts, whatever its scheme. An unparsable authority is read by hand."""
    # `<transport>::<address>` names a remote helper for an arbitrary address (`https::https://t@host`): git
    # splits at the FIRST `::` and hands everything after it to git-remote-<transport> as one argument, so a
    # second prefix or an ssh-looking inner scheme changes nothing (rounds 23 and 24): no parsing, any `@` or `?`
    head, sep, rest = url.partition("::")
    if sep and head and re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9+.-]+", head):
        return "@" in rest or "?" in rest
    if "://" not in url:
        return False
    scheme = url.partition("://")[0].lower()
    try:
        parts = urlsplit(url)
        netloc, password, query = parts.netloc, parts.password, parts.query
    except ValueError:
        rest = url.partition("://")[2]
        netloc, password, query = rest.split("/", 1)[0], None, (rest.partition("?")[2] if "?" in rest else "")
    userinfo = "@" in netloc
    has_password = password is not None or (userinfo and ":" in netloc.rpartition("@")[0])
    if scheme in ("ssh", "git+ssh", "ssh+git"):
        return has_password
    return userinfo or has_password or bool(query)


def ls_remote_refs(url: str) -> tuple[dict[str, str], dict[str, tuple[str, str]], int] | None:
    """(branches {name: tip oid}, tags {name: (oid, effective oid)}, the number of advertised entries dropped
    as invalid), None when `ls-remote` failed. The effective oid of a tag is its peeled commit (`^{}` entry)
    when it is annotated, else the oid itself. An entry is dropped when its name fails `valid_branch_name`
    (the same rule for tags), its oid is not 40 or 64 lowercase hex digits or is all zeros, the line is not
    `<oid> TAB refs/heads/<name>` or `refs/tags/<name>`, or it is a peeled entry with no tag line."""
    env = git_env(network=True)
    # the temp dir's parent is a ceiling: git must not discover a repository above the neutral dir
    env["GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES"] = str(Path(neutral_cwd()).parent)
    rc, out = run(["git", *GIT_SAFE, "ls-remote", "--heads", "--tags", "--", "origin"], timeout=120,
                  cwd=neutral_cwd(), env=origin_url_env(env, url))
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    heads: dict[str, str] = {}
    plain: dict[str, str] = {}
    peeled: dict[str, str] = {}
    invalid = 0
    for ln in out.splitlines():
        if not ln.strip():
            continue
        oid, _, ref = ln.partition("\t")
        if not _OID.fullmatch(oid) or not oid.strip("0"):
            invalid += 1
            continue
        if ref.startswith("refs/heads/"):
            name = ref[len("refs/heads/"):]
            if valid_branch_name(name):
                heads[name] = oid
                continue
        elif ref.startswith("refs/tags/"):
            name = ref[len("refs/tags/"):]
            is_peeled = name.endswith("^{}")
            name = name[:-3] if is_peeled else name
            if valid_branch_name(name):
                (peeled if is_peeled else plain)[name] = oid
                continue
        invalid += 1
    invalid += sum(1 for name in peeled if name not in plain)
    tags = {name: (oid, peeled.get(name, oid)) for name, oid in plain.items()}
    return heads, tags, invalid


_remote_cache: dict[tuple[str, str], tuple] = {}


def local_origin_refs(path: Path) -> dict[str, str] | None:
    rc, out = git(path, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:strip=3) %(objectname)", "refs/remotes/origin")
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    return dict(ln.split() for ln in out.splitlines() if len(ln.split()) == 2)


def local_tag_refs(path: Path) -> dict[str, str] | None:
    """{tag name: object id (the tag object for an annotated tag)} of the local tags."""
    rc, out = git(path, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:strip=2) %(objectname)", "refs/tags")
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    return dict(ln.split() for ln in out.splitlines() if len(ln.split()) == 2)


def origin_branches(path: Path, remote: bool) -> tuple[dict[str, str] | None, bool, int,
                                                       dict[str, tuple[str, str]], bool]:
    """(branch name -> tip sha on origin, whether `ls-remote` answered, advertised entries dropped as
    invalid names or oids, tag name -> (oid, effective oid) on origin, whether origin's url holds a
    password: then nothing is asked of origin, with or without --remote, and the url is kept nowhere).

    With --remote the tips come from `ls-remote`; when origin does not answer they come from the local
    remote refs instead (adversary round 4: None read as "unknown" and hid unpushed work), the second
    value is False and the caller flags the checkout `origin unreachable`; without --remote the second
    value is False too. No tags then. None: no origin, or no readable local refs."""
    rc, _ = git(path, "remote", "get-url", "origin")
    if rc != 0:
        return (None, False, 0, {}, False)
    # Worktrees share a git dir but may carry worktree-scoped remote.origin.url values
    # (extensions.worktreeConfig): the cache key is (git dir, the url ls-remote is given), never the
    # git dir alone (Codex pass 9)
    url = queried_origin_url(path)
    rc, common = git(path, "rev-parse", "--git-common-dir")
    # the url itself is never a key: its sha256 prefix stands in (it may hold a password)
    key = (str((path / common.strip()).resolve()) if rc == 0 and common.strip() else str(path),
           hashlib.sha256((url or "").encode()).hexdigest()[:16])
    if key not in _remote_cache:
        credential = bool(url) and url_holds_credential(url)  # never queried, never kept
        answer = (ls_remote_refs(url) if remote and url and not credential and git_takes_env_config()
                  else None)
        _remote_cache[key] = ((answer[0], True, answer[2], answer[1], False) if answer is not None
                              else (local_origin_refs(path), False, 0, {}, credential))
    return _remote_cache[key]


def skipped_record(path: Path, reason: str, keys: list[str] | None = None) -> dict:
    return {
        "path": str(path), "branch": "?", "modified": None, "untracked": None,
        "untracked_sample": [], "modified_sample": [], "upstream": None, "unpushed": None,
        "ahead_of_origin": None, "stashes": None, "has_origin": None, "on_origin": "unknown",
        "local_only_commits": None, "local_only_verified": None, "invalid_origin_refs": 0,
        "local_only_branches": [], "local_only_tags": [], "local_only_branches_uncounted": 0,
        "config_keys": keys or [], "flags": [reason], "verdict": reason,
    }


def incomplete_record(path: Path, call: str, rc: int) -> dict:
    """A git call failed (rc 124 = timed out): the checkout's state is unknown, never `clean and pushed`."""
    return skipped_record(path, f"{INCOMPLETE_PREFIX}{call} rc={rc}")


def local_only_count(path: Path, revs: list[str], tips: list[str],
                     live: bool = False, has_origin: bool = True) -> tuple[int | None, int]:
    """(commits reachable from `revs` and from no origin ref, rc); None when git failed.

    Without a live answer origin's refs are the local `refs/remotes/origin/*`. With one (`live`, --remote
    and `ls-remote` answered) they are `tips` alone: a local tracking ref proves nothing about a live
    branch (origin may have force-pushed it) and one of a deleted branch holds nothing there (Codex
    passes 9 and 11). Tips whose objects are not here are ignored (`--ignore-missing`): the count then
    only overcounts, and the caller flags it unverified. With no origin remote (`has_origin` false) a
    leftover `refs/remotes/origin/*` excludes nothing (Codex pass 18)."""
    argv = ["rev-list", "--count", *revs, "--not"]
    if not live and has_origin:
        argv.append("--remotes=origin")
    if tips:
        argv += ["--ignore-missing", *tips]
    rc, n = git(path, *argv)
    return (int(n) if rc == 0 and n.strip().isdigit() else None), rc


def scan_checkout(path: Path, remote: bool, fetch: bool = False) -> dict:
    keys = unsafe_config(path)  # before any other git call: reading config runs nothing
    if keys is None:
        return skipped_record(path, SKIP_UNREADABLE)
    if "core.worktree" in keys:
        return skipped_record(path, SKIP_WORKTREE, keys)
    if keys:
        return skipped_record(path, f"skipped: unrecognised config {keys[0]}", keys)
    rc, top = git(path, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if rc != 0 or not top.strip():
        rc_dir, _ = git(path, "rev-parse", "--git-dir")
        if rc_dir != 0 and (path / ".git" / "HEAD").is_file():
            return skipped_record(path, SKIP_HEAD_UNREADABLE)  # git does not recognise the repository
        return skipped_record(path, SKIP_NO_WORKTREE)
    if Path(top.strip()).resolve() != path.resolve():
        return skipped_record(path, SKIP_WORKTREE)
    common = git_common_dir(path)
    has_submodules, bad_key = submodule_config_check(path, common)
    if bad_key:
        return skipped_record(path, f"skipped: submodule config unrecognised {bad_key}", [bad_key])
    rc, branch = git(path, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD")
    unborn = False
    if rc != 0:
        rc_branch = rc
        # A repository with no commit yet fails here and is still a valid, scannable checkout; one that
        # has refs but whose HEAD names none of them is damaged, not unborn (adversary round 4, 2).
        rc_head, _ = git(path, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "HEAD")
        if rc_head == 1:
            rc_refs, refs = git(path, "for-each-ref", "--count=1", "refs/heads", "refs/tags")
            if rc_refs == 0 and not refs.strip():
                rc, branch = git(path, "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "--short", "HEAD")
                unborn = rc == 0
        if not unborn:
            return incomplete_record(path, "rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD", rc_branch)
    branch = branch.strip()
    detached = branch == "HEAD"
    # A git call that fails or times out must never read as "nothing found" (adversary round 3, 1).
    rc, status = git(path, "status", "--porcelain", "--ignore-submodules=all",
                     "--untracked-files=normal", timeout=STATUS_TIMEOUT)
    if rc != 0:
        return incomplete_record(path, "status", rc)
    lines = status.splitlines()
    untracked = [ln[3:] for ln in lines if ln.startswith("??")]
    modified = [ln[3:] for ln in lines if not ln.startswith("??")]
    rc, up = git(path, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "--symbolic-full-name", "@{u}")
    upstream = up.strip() if rc == 0 else None
    unpushed = None
    if upstream and not unborn:
        rc, n = git(path, "rev-list", "--count", "@{u}..HEAD")
        if rc != 0 or not n.strip().isdigit():
            return incomplete_record(path, "rev-list --count @{u}..HEAD", rc)
        unpushed = int(n)
    # `git remote get-url` exits 2 with "error: No such remote 'origin'" (observed, git's own answer) when
    # origin is not configured; a timeout (124), a damaged config (128) or anything else is unknown, never
    # "no origin", which would turn the checkout into an orphan instead of `scan incomplete` (Codex pass 8)
    rc, _ = git(path, "remote", "get-url", "origin")
    if rc not in (0, NO_SUCH_REMOTE_RC):
        return incomplete_record(path, "remote get-url origin", rc)
    has_origin = rc == 0
    # Without an origin remote, leftover `refs/remotes/origin/*` (the config edited instead of `git remote
    # remove`) prove nothing: every commit is local-only (Codex pass 18 on PR 27)
    not_origin = ["--not", "--remotes=origin"] if has_origin else []
    ahead_of_origin = 0
    if not unborn:
        rc, n = git(path, "rev-list", "--count", "HEAD", *not_origin)
        if rc != 0 or not n.strip().isdigit():
            return incomplete_record(path, "rev-list --count HEAD --not --remotes=origin", rc)
        ahead_of_origin = int(n)
    stashes = stash_count(path, common)
    if stashes is None:
        return incomplete_record(path, "stash list", 1)
    branches, live, invalid_refs, tags, credential = (origin_branches(path, remote) if has_origin
                                                      else (None, False, 0, {}, False))
    origin_unreachable = bool(remote and has_origin and not live)
    on_origin = False if not has_origin else (None if branches is None or detached
                                              else branch in branches)
    # Local origin/* refs can be stale: a branch whose origin tip is HEAD, or contains HEAD,
    # has nothing unpushed whatever the local remote-tracking refs say.
    tip = branches.get(branch) if branches and on_origin else None
    tip_unverified = False
    if tip and ahead_of_origin:
        # count again against the real origin tip: HEAD may already be inside it
        def ahead_of_tip():
            rc, n = git(path, "rev-list", "--count", "HEAD", "--not", "--remotes=origin", tip)
            return int(n) if rc == 0 and n.strip().isdigit() else None
        n = ahead_of_tip()
        if n is None and fetch:  # the tip's objects are not here: download them, write no refs
            if not credential:  # `fetch` hands the configured url to its transport helper: not with a password
                git(path, "-c", "maintenance.auto=false", "-c", "gc.auto=0", "fetch", "--quiet",
                    "--no-auto-maintenance", "--no-write-fetch-head", "--no-tags", "--refmap=",
                    "--no-recurse-submodules", "--no-prune", "--",
                    "origin", f"refs/heads/{branch}", timeout=120, network=True)
            n = ahead_of_tip()
        if n is None:
            tip_unverified = True  # origin has a tip we cannot compare with HEAD
        else:
            ahead_of_origin = n
    # Commits on no origin ref, over HEAD and every local branch (adversary round 4, 1): the verdict
    # `clean and pushed` needs this to be 0 whatever `on_origin` says. ls-remote tips (when origin
    # answered) are excluded. A live tip whose objects are not here excludes nothing, and neither does
    # the stale tracking ref of that branch: origin may have force-pushed it, and the ancestry check
    # needs the tip's objects (Codex pass 11). With --fetch those tips are downloaded first; whatever
    # is still missing makes the count an overcount (`local_only_verified` false) and the verdict not
    # clean.
    # Every live head and tag tip (an annotated tag: its object and its peeled commit) is excluded; the
    # tip count for `origin tips not fetched` takes one commit per head and per tag.
    tips = (sorted({*branches.values(), *(o for pair in tags.values() for o in pair)})[:MAX_TIPS]
            if branches is not None and live else [])
    check_tips = (sorted({*branches.values(), *(eff for _, eff in tags.values())})[:MAX_TIPS]
                  if branches is not None and live else [])
    tips_missing = 0

    def tip_state() -> tuple[int | None, set[str], int]:
        """(live tips whose object is missing here, present tips that are not commits, rc). A tag on a tree
        or blob reaches no commit: nothing to exclude, nothing to fetch, never "missing" for good
        (git.git `junio-gpg-pub`, linux `v2.6.11-tree`; adversary round 21, 2). None when git failed."""
        rc, out = git(path, "cat-file", "--batch-check", stdin_text="".join(f"{o}\n" for o in check_tips))
        rows = out.splitlines()
        if rc != 0 or len(rows) != len(check_tips):
            return None, set(), rc
        kinds = [r.split() for r in rows]
        return (sum(1 for k in kinds if k[-1] == "missing"),
                {o for o, k in zip(check_tips, kinds) if len(k) >= 2 and k[1] != "commit" and k[-1] != "missing"}, rc)

    if check_tips:
        tips_missing, noncommit, rc = tip_state()
        if tips_missing is None:
            return incomplete_record(path, "cat-file --batch-check", rc)
        if tips_missing and fetch and not credential:
            # only a ref whose local copy differs from its live tip can be missing
            local_refs = local_origin_refs(path) or {}
            local_tags = local_tag_refs(path) or {}
            stale = ([f"refs/heads/{b}" for b, sha in sorted(branches.items()) if local_refs.get(b) != sha]
                     + [f"refs/tags/{t}" for t, (oid, eff) in sorted(tags.items())
                        if local_tags.get(t) != oid and eff not in noncommit])
            if stale:
                # belt and braces: `ls_remote_refs` already dropped such names; a `:` or whitespace in
                # one would make it a `<src>:<dst>` refspec that writes a local ref
                refspecs = [r for r in stale if ":" not in r and not any(c.isspace() for c in r)]
                git(path, "-c", "maintenance.auto=false", "-c", "gc.auto=0", "fetch", "--quiet",
                    "--no-auto-maintenance", "--no-write-fetch-head", "--no-tags", "--refmap=",
                    "--no-recurse-submodules", "--no-prune", "--", "origin",
                    *refspecs[:MAX_BRANCH_COUNTS], timeout=120, network=True)
                tips_missing, _, rc = tip_state()
                if tips_missing is None:
                    return incomplete_record(path, "cat-file --batch-check", rc)
    local_only: int | None = 0
    local_only_branches: list[dict] = []
    local_only_tags: list[dict] = []
    branches_uncounted = 0
    if not unborn:
        local_only, rc = local_only_count(path, ["HEAD", "--branches", "--tags"], tips, live, has_origin)
        if local_only is None:
            return incomplete_record(path, "rev-list --count HEAD --branches --tags --not --remotes=origin", rc)
        if local_only:
            if detached:
                n, rc = local_only_count(path, ["HEAD"], tips, live, has_origin)
                if n is None:
                    return incomplete_record(path, "rev-list --count HEAD --not --remotes=origin", rc)
                if n:
                    local_only_branches.append({"branch": "HEAD", "count": n})
            rc, out = git(path, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:strip=2)", "refs/heads")
            if rc != 0:
                return incomplete_record(path, "for-each-ref refs/heads", rc)
            names = out.splitlines()
            for name in names[:MAX_BRANCH_COUNTS]:
                n, rc = local_only_count(path, [f"refs/heads/{name}"], tips, live, has_origin)
                if n is None:
                    return incomplete_record(path, f"rev-list --count refs/heads/{name} "
                                                   "--not --remotes=origin", rc)
                if n:
                    local_only_branches.append({"branch": name, "count": n})
            rc, out = git(path, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:strip=2)", "refs/tags")
            if rc != 0:
                return incomplete_record(path, "for-each-ref refs/tags", rc)
            tag_names = out.splitlines()
            tag_budget = max(MAX_BRANCH_COUNTS - len(names[:MAX_BRANCH_COUNTS]), 0)  # one cap for both lists
            for name in tag_names[:tag_budget]:
                n, rc = local_only_count(path, [f"refs/tags/{name}"], tips, live, has_origin)
                if n is None:
                    return incomplete_record(path, f"rev-list --count refs/tags/{name} "
                                                   "--not --remotes=origin", rc)
                if n:
                    local_only_tags.append({"tag": name, "count": n})
            branches_uncounted = (max(len(names) - MAX_BRANCH_COUNTS, 0)
                                  + max(len(tag_names) - tag_budget, 0))
    if live and on_origin is False and ahead_of_origin and not unborn:
        # the branch is not on origin but its commits may be, under a live tag or a branch with no local tracking
        # ref: count HEAD against the live tips, like the local-only count, before calling it an orphan (Codex pass 19)
        n, rc = local_only_count(path, ["HEAD"], tips, live, has_origin)
        if n is None:
            return incomplete_record(path, "rev-list --count HEAD --not <live tips>", rc)
        ahead_of_origin = n
    flags = []
    if unpushed or (on_origin and (ahead_of_origin or 0) > 0):
        flags.append("unpushed commits")  # includes a branch on origin with no upstream set
    if on_origin is False and (ahead_of_origin or 0) > 0 and not unpushed:
        flags.append("orphan branch")
    if local_only:
        if not flags:
            flags.append("unpushed commits")  # only on another branch, or origin not known
        if detached:
            flags.append("detached")
        elif on_origin is None:
            flags.append("origin unknown")
        entries = ([f"{b['branch']} {b['count']}" for b in local_only_branches]
                   + [f"tag {t['tag']} {t['count']}" for t in local_only_tags])
        shown = ", ".join(entries[:5])
        more = len(entries) - 5
        flags.append("local-only commits: " + (shown or f"{local_only} in total")
                     + (f", +{more} more" if more > 0 else ""))
    if credential and remote:
        flags.append(ORIGIN_CREDENTIAL)  # not queried: unreachable in every respect
    elif origin_unreachable:
        flags.append(ORIGIN_UNREACHABLE)
    if tip_unverified:
        flags.append("origin tip not fetched")
    if tips_missing:
        flags.append(f"{TIPS_NOT_FETCHED}{tips_missing}")
    if invalid_refs:
        flags.append(f"{INVALID_REFS}{invalid_refs}")
    if not has_origin:
        flags.append("no origin remote")
    if lines:
        flags.append("dirty")
    if has_submodules:
        flags.append(SUBMODULES_FLAG)
    if stashes:
        flags.append("stash")
    primary = next((f for f in flags if f not in INFO_FLAGS), "clean and pushed")
    return {
        "path": str(path), "branch": branch, "modified": len(modified), "untracked": len(untracked),
        "untracked_sample": untracked[:8], "modified_sample": modified[:8],
        "upstream": upstream, "unpushed": unpushed if upstream else "no upstream",
        "ahead_of_origin": ahead_of_origin, "stashes": stashes,
        "local_only_commits": local_only,
        # only a live `--remote` answer verifies anything: true when no live tip is missing, false when
        # one is or when `--remote` asked and origin did not answer (a failed query is unverified, not "not
        # asked"), null when --remote was not given (the count then trusts the local refs)
        "local_only_verified": False if origin_unreachable else ((tips_missing == 0) if live else None),
        "invalid_origin_refs": invalid_refs,
        "local_only_branches": local_only_branches,
        "local_only_tags": local_only_tags,
        "local_only_branches_uncounted": branches_uncounted,
        "has_origin": has_origin,
        "on_origin": "unknown" if on_origin is None else on_origin,
        "flags": flags, "verdict": primary,
    }


def scan_git(roots: list[Path], remote: bool, fetch: bool = False) -> list[dict]:
    seen, out = set(), []
    for root in roots:
        unreadable: list[tuple[Path, str]] = []
        found = find_checkouts(root, unreadable)
        for bad, err in unreadable:
            real = bad.resolve()
            if real in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(real)
            rec = skipped_record(real, f"{INCOMPLETE_PREFIX}scandir {err}")
            rec["found_via"] = str(bad)
            out.append(rec)
        for co in found:
            real = co.resolve()
            if real in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(real)
            rec = scan_checkout(real, remote, fetch)
            rec["found_via"] = str(co)
            out.append(rec)
    return out


def human(n: int) -> str:
    v = float(n)
    for unit in ("B", "K", "M", "G", "T"):
        if v < 1024 or unit == "T":
            return f"{v:.0f}{unit}" if unit == "B" else f"{v:.1f}{unit}"
        v /= 1024
    return str(n)


def summarize(result: dict) -> dict:
    s = {}
    bt = result.get("bigteam")
    if bt:
        verdicts: dict[str, int] = {}
        for t in bt["tasks"]:
            verdicts[t["verdict"]] = verdicts.get(t["verdict"], 0) + 1
        total = sum(t["bytes"] for t in bt["tasks"])
        uniq = sum(t["unique_bytes"] for t in bt["tasks"])
        s["bigteam"] = {"tasks": len(bt["tasks"]), "verdicts": verdicts, "total_bytes": total,
                        "unique_bytes": uniq, "scratch_bytes": total - uniq,
                        "incomplete": sum(1 for t in bt["tasks"]
                                          if t["verdict"].startswith(INCOMPLETE_PREFIX))}
    if "checkouts" in result:
        v: dict[str, int] = {}
        for c in result["checkouts"]:
            v[c["verdict"]] = v.get(c["verdict"], 0) + 1
        s["checkouts"] = {
            "count": len(result["checkouts"]), "verdicts": v,
            "with_unpushed_work": sum(1 for c in result["checkouts"]
                                      if "unpushed commits" in c["flags"]
                                      or "orphan branch" in c["flags"]),
            "with_local_only_commits": sum(1 for c in result["checkouts"]
                                           if (c.get("local_only_commits") or 0) > 0),
            "with_untracked": sum(1 for c in result["checkouts"] if c["untracked"]),
            "with_stash": sum(1 for c in result["checkouts"] if c["stashes"]),
            "skipped": sum(1 for c in result["checkouts"] if c["verdict"].startswith("skipped")),
            "incomplete": sum(1 for c in result["checkouts"]
                              if c["verdict"].startswith(INCOMPLETE_PREFIX))}
    return s


_CTRL = re.compile(r"[\x00-\x1f\x7f]")


def esc(value) -> str:
    """One markdown table cell: `|` and backticks escaped, `<`, `>`, `&` HTML-escaped, every control character
    (newline, a bare CR that CommonMark ends a line at, tab, ...) shown as backslash-n, -r, -t or -xNN text."""
    if value is None:
        return "-"
    text = str(value).replace("&", "&amp;").replace("<", "&lt;").replace(">", "&gt;")
    text = text.replace("|", "\\|").replace("`", "\\`")
    named = {"\n": "\\n", "\r": "\\r", "\t": "\\t"}
    return _CTRL.sub(lambda m: named.get(m.group()) or f"\\x{ord(m.group()):02x}", text)


def markdown(result: dict) -> str:
    out = []
    bt = result.get("bigteam")
    if bt:
        out += ["| task | size | newest | CLAIM | reports (.done) | open jobs | verdict |",
                "|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|"]
        for t in sorted(bt["tasks"], key=lambda t: -t["bytes"]):
            reps = ", ".join(f"{esc(Path(r['path']).name)}{'' if r['done'] else ' (no .done)'}"
                             for r in t["reports"][:3]) + (" +%d" % (len(t["reports"]) - 3)
                                                            if len(t["reports"]) > 3 else "")
            when = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d", time.localtime(t["newest_mtime"]))
            out.append(f"| {esc(t['task'])} | {human(t['bytes'])} | {when} | {esc(t['claim'])} | "
                       f"{reps or '-'} | {len(t['open_job_records'])} | {esc(t['verdict'])} |")
        out.append("")
    if "checkouts" in result:
        out += ["| path | branch | modified | untracked | unpushed | stash | on origin | verdict |",
                "|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|"]
        for c in result["checkouts"]:
            also = ", ".join(c["flags"][1:])
            out.append(f"| {esc(c['path'])} | {esc(c['branch'])} | {esc(c['modified'])} | "
                       f"{esc(c['untracked'])} | {esc(c['unpushed'])} | {esc(c['stashes'])} | "
                       f"{esc(c['on_origin'])} | {esc(c['verdict'])}{esc(' + ' + also) if also else ''}"
                       f" |")
    return "\n".join(out)


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="ade-scan-state", description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
    ap.add_argument("--bigteam", type=Path, help="bigteam root, one dir per task")
    ap.add_argument("--ref-root", type=Path, action="append", default=[],
                    help="where a task name counts as referenced (repeatable)")
    ap.add_argument("--exclude", action="append", default=[],
                    help="rg glob (no leading !) to ignore under --ref-root, added to the built-in "
                         "%s (the scan docs)" % ", ".join(DEFAULT_EXCLUDES))
    ap.add_argument("--git-root", type=Path, action="append", default=[],
                    help="a dir whose checkouts (up to 2 levels down, repositories nested in a checkout "
                         "included) are scanned; symlinks under it are not followed (repeatable)")
    ap.add_argument("--remote", action="store_true",
                    help="ask origin with `git ls-remote --heads --tags origin` (network call, run outside the "
                         "checkout; the first `remote.origin.url`, a path resolved against the checkout, "
                         "reaches git through GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY_0/VALUE_0 (git 2.31+), never argv; an "
                         "older git is not asked and the checkout is `origin unreachable`); an origin url "
                         "with a password, or a username or query string on a non-ssh scheme (a `<transport>::` prefix stripped first), is never queried (`origin url holds a credential: not queried`, "
                         "treated like `origin unreachable`); "
                         "its live tips, not the local refs/remotes/origin/* (stale after a "
                         "branch is deleted there), then decide what is pushed; an origin that does not "
                         "answer makes the checkout `origin unreachable`, never clean and pushed; origin's "
                         "answer is trusted as given: a lying origin can hide any local commit by naming "
                         "it as a branch, tag or peeled tip")
    ap.add_argument("--fetch", action="store_true",
                    help="with --remote: `git fetch origin refs/heads/<branch>` (120 s limit) when the "
                         "origin tip's objects are missing locally, to compare it with HEAD; downloads "
                         "the branch's objects, writes no refs and no FETCH_HEAD")
    ap.add_argument("--unique-max-bytes", type=int, default=5 * 1024 * 1024)
    ap.add_argument("--top", type=int, default=10, help="largest bigteam dirs to break down")
    fmt = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    fmt.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    fmt.add_argument("--markdown", action="store_true")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if not args.bigteam and not args.git_root:
        ap.error("give --bigteam and/or --git-root")
    for root in args.git_root:
        if not root.is_dir():
            ap.error(f"--git-root {root} is not a directory")
    for root in args.ref_root:
        # a root that is missing, unlistable or not searchable (r-- is listable) would silently narrow the
        # reference search, so a task cited only there would read `unique` (Codex pass 6)
        if not root.is_dir():
            ap.error(f"--ref-root {root} is not a directory")
        if not os.access(root, os.R_OK | os.X_OK):
            ap.error(f"--ref-root {root} cannot be searched")
    result: dict = {"scanned_at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z")}
    if args.bigteam:
        if not args.bigteam.is_dir():
            ap.error(f"--bigteam {args.bigteam} is not a directory")
        try:
            with os.scandir(args.bigteam):
                pass
        except OSError as exc:
            ap.error(f"--bigteam {args.bigteam} cannot be listed ({errno_name(exc)})")
        result["bigteam"] = scan_bigteam(args.bigteam, args.ref_root, args.exclude,
                                         args.unique_max_bytes, args.top)
    if args.git_root:
        result["checkouts"] = scan_git(args.git_root, args.remote, args.fetch)
    result["summary"] = summarize(result)
    if args.json:
        json.dump(result, sys.stdout, indent=1)
        print()
    elif args.markdown:
        print(markdown(result))
    else:
        print(json.dumps(result["summary"], indent=1))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
